1
00:00:02,220 --> 00:00:08,090
let's look now at direct memory access so
we have discussed interrupts and now we know

2
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:14,480
that the CPU can be working on something and let
the controller of a device send the signal when

3
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:20,769
something happens like some output has been
written disk or some input is available

4
00:00:20,769 --> 00:00:23,769
for the CPU and so on

5
00:00:23,769 --> 00:00:32,119
so there is an extra mechanism that also is
supported hardware that takes away

6
00:00:32,119 --> 00:00:37,250
even more job from the CPU when it comes to
transferring large amounts of data to specific

7
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:43,000
devices and that is direct memory access

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,710
the idea is that if you have to transfer a big
chunk of data from main memory to the disk

9
00:00:48,710 --> 00:01:00,190
for example since you have to send them in
specific chunks depending on what is the size

10
00:01:00,190 --> 00:01:04,210
of the buffers you have at the controller
and the device to transfer data

11
00:01:04,210 --> 00:01:10,470
having the CPU do this job would even if it
is optimized with interrupts it would still

12
00:01:10,470 --> 00:01:17,390
consume time that the CPU can actually spend
running other processes

13
00:01:17,390 --> 00:01:22,330
so what happens in modern hardware is that you
have a dedicated controller that is the dma

14
00:01:22,330 --> 00:01:26,080
controller the direct memory access that
you can just think of as

15
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:32,310
an extra controller that takes care of transferring
stuff/data from memory to devices or

16
00:01:32,310 --> 00:01:39,560
from devices to memory and then notify the
CPU when the transfer is complete so it's an

17
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:45,250
extra helper if you want at the hardware level
that takes care of transferring data from

18
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:50,020
the main memory to the devices in both directions
depending of

19
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:59,100
course on the type of device so basically
when you initiate when you want to initiate

20
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:06,530
the transfer of using direct memory access
the host writes in a specific portion or address

21
00:02:06,530 --> 00:02:14,470
in memory that the DMA has access specifying
basically what information needs to be copied

22
00:02:14,470 --> 00:02:18,340
and what is the destination and then the
controller of the DMA takes care

23
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:24,840
of actually doing the copy /
the transferring of data

24
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:32,930
you need of course an handshaking protocol
routine also when you want to coordinate with

25
00:02:32,930 --> 00:02:40,150
the DMA this is about of course
the controllers that need to transfer data

26
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:45,090
using that the DMA controller for example
a disk that wants to

27
00:02:45,090 --> 00:02:52,770
use the DMA controller and also in this case you
have dedicated lines for requests and acknowledgment

28
00:02:52,770 --> 00:03:03,980
from the DMA controller so I
think the easiest is if I show you this image

29
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:13,200
here so let's say the CPU is running 
a certain process and then at a certain point

30
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:14,739
it needs to transfer data

31
00:03:14,739 --> 00:03:23,640
to the disk a big chunk of data to the disk so
this is step one what is happening in your

32
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:35,140
computer modern hardware is that
the CPU will send the request to the controller

33
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:40,950
to transfer the desired amount of data from
a disk to main memory

34
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:49,269
after that the controller of the disk will
actually coordinate with the DMA controller

35
00:03:49,269 --> 00:03:56,970
the direct memory access controller here
and send the data to disk controller so basically

36
00:03:56,970 --> 00:04:04,470
the DMA is now acting instead of the
CPU when it comes to taking the data that

37
00:04:04,470 --> 00:04:07,819
comes from the disk and placedthat into memory
at a

38
00:04:07,819 --> 00:04:13,489
specific buffer so the DMA takes care of
this work while the CPU is able to do other

39
00:04:13,489 --> 00:04:22,060
things run other processes it will take
a while for the disk for the controller of

40
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:24,950
the disk to send the bytes to the DMA controller

41
00:04:24,950 --> 00:04:32,650
and the controller will keep adding to the
buffer here in the main memory until of course

42
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:39,840
everything has been written to memory then
the interrupt that is sent to the CPU to say

43
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:45,430
the data you wanted from the disk is now
in memory will be actually generated by the

44
00:04:45,430 --> 00:04:46,740
DMA controller

45
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:55,781
so the CPU reaches the point in a process
in which a system call says you need to transfer

46
00:04:55,781 --> 00:05:02,110
something from the disk then the controller
of the disk communicates with the DMA so the

47
00:05:02,110 --> 00:05:04,070
CPU goes on doing other things

48
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:11,500
and the final interrupt that will create a
context switch eventually so that the CPU can

49
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:16,590
go and start running again the process that
was waiting for the data comes from the DMA

50
00:05:16,590 --> 00:05:18,500
controller in this case

